<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The Windows installer (ADR-0019).

  The package delivers one file into `Program Files` and then calls the Client's own
  `service install` (ADR-0019), which builds the layout and the state directory under
  `%ProgramData%\opamp-fleet`. That division is ADR-0019 clause 8: `Program Files` is
  `TrustedInstaller`-owned and read-only once installation finishes, and the layout is rewritten at
  runtime by the daemon — the self-update stages a new version directory and swings `current`. It
  is the same line the `.deb` and `.rpm` draw between `/usr/libexec` and `/opt`. The MSI therefore
  passes **no root at all** and takes the platform defaults, which is also what makes a Windows
  host installed from the archive and one installed by this package identical.

  It registers no service of its own. An MSI `ServiceInstall` element would write a registration
  knowing nothing of ADR-0019's service name and display name, nor of ADR-0018's SCM recovery
  actions, both of which `service install` sets through calls the `service-manager` backend cannot
  make. There is one install path on four operating systems and this is not a second one.

  Built by `wix build` with the UI extension; `Version` and `BinaryPath` come in as -d arguments
  from the release workflow, so the version in the MSI is the one the binary reports (ADR-0012).
-->
<Wix xmlns="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs">

  <Package Name="OpAMP Fleet Agent"
           Manufacturer="OpAMP Fleet"
           Version="$(Version)"
           Language="1033"
           Codepage="1252"
           Scope="perMachine"
           Compressed="yes"
           InstallerVersion="500"
           UpgradeCode="0CC090C9-169E-493A-B497-760713C83DDC">

    <!--
      The UpgradeCode above is the product's permanent identity: it is how Windows Installer knows
      1.2.4 is an upgrade of 1.2.3 rather than a second product beside it. NEVER change it. A new
      GUID here strands every host that already carries the old one — they would keep the old
      version installed forever and gain a duplicate entry in Add/Remove Programs. The ProductCode
      is deliberately left to be generated per build, which is what MajorUpgrade requires.
    -->
    <MajorUpgrade AllowSameVersionUpgrades="yes"
                  DowngradeErrorMessage="A newer version of [ProductName] is already installed. Uninstall it first to install this one." />

    <MediaTemplate EmbedCab="yes" />

    <SummaryInformation Description="OpAMP Fleet Agent — places a machine under OpAMP management" />

    <!--
      Every answer the dialogs collect is a public property (all uppercase) and Secure, and that is
      not cosmetic:

        * public, because Windows Installer resets private properties when execution crosses from
          the UI sequence into the execute sequence — a value typed into a dialog would never reach
          the custom action below;
        * Secure, because that is what lets an administrator set them on the command line, which is
          how Intune, Group Policy and SCCM will deploy this:

              msiexec /i supervisor_1.2.3_windows_amd64.msi /qn ^
                INSTALLFOLDER="C:\Program Files\opamp-fleet" ^
                ENDPOINT="wss://fleet.example.com/v1/opamp" ^
                SELFUPDATE=0

      ENDPOINT is empty by default in the execute sequence: an empty one is a legal answer meaning
      "configure later", and it selects the second custom action below. Only the interactive dialog
      is prefilled with the development default (ADR-0019) — the SetProperty runs in the UI
      sequence alone, so a silent deploy (`/qn`, Intune, Group Policy) that names no ENDPOINT still
      defers configuration instead of pinning every host it touches to localhost, which is the
      thousand-hosts state ADR-0019 refuses to manufacture. A value given on the command line wins
      over the prefill, and a maintenance run never re-answers a question the first install already
      settled. The value is the development Server in its `http://` form — deliberately not the
      loader's own `ws://` default: the scheme selects the transport, and ADR-0019 records the
      operator's choice of HTTP for the click-through install. `tests/msi_exe_command.rs` holds
      the value to the loader's endpoint rule and to this string.
    -->
    <Property Id="ENDPOINT" Secure="yes" />

    <!--
      The self-update consent (ADR-0018). Unlike ENDPOINT's prefill this default is set in *both*
      sequences, because default-on is the decision rather than a convenience for the click-through
      path: a silent `/qn` deploy that names nothing gets a fleet-updatable Client, which is the
      point. An administrator withdraws it the same way the dialog's cleared checkbox does:

          msiexec /i supervisor_1.2.3_windows_amd64.msi /qn SELFUPDATE=0

      Both spellings of "off" have to work, and this is the MSI trap worth naming: a condition of
      the bare property name is true for *any* non-empty value, so `SELFUPDATE=0` — which is what an
      administrator will type — would read as ON. The dialog's checkbox clears the property to empty
      instead. Hence the two-armed condition below, which is what SELFUPDATEFLAG is computed from
      rather than repeated at each use.

      SELFUPDATEFLAG is computed in the **execute** sequence alone, and deliberately not in "both":
      the only readers are the deferred custom actions, whose formatted Target is resolved as the
      installation script is written — and `InstallInitialize`, the point a SetProperty feeding a
      deferred action belongs before, exists in the execute sequence only. A `Sequence="both"` here
      would ask the UI sequence to schedule against an action it does not have. The checkbox's own
      value reaches the execute sequence on its own, because SELFUPDATE is public and Secure.
    -->
    <Property Id="SELFUPDATE" Value="1" Secure="yes" />
    <!--
      `SELFUPDATE=0` given to a run that shows the UI is normalised to empty first, so the checkbox
      renders unchecked instead of checked-but-not-honoured: a checkbox is checked whenever its
      property is non-empty, and "0" is non-empty. `{}` is the formatted field that yields nothing,
      which is how a property is cleared. UI sequence only — a silent install draws no checkbox, and
      there the two-armed condition below already reads "0" correctly.
    -->
    <SetProperty Id="SELFUPDATE"
                 Value="{}"
                 Sequence="ui"
                 After="LaunchConditions"
                 Condition="SELFUPDATE=&quot;0&quot;" />
    <SetProperty Id="SELFUPDATEFLAG"
                 Value=" --no-self-update"
                 Sequence="execute"
                 Before="InstallInitialize"
                 Condition="NOT SELFUPDATE OR SELFUPDATE=&quot;0&quot;" />
    <SetProperty Id="ENDPOINT"
                 Value="wss://127.0.0.1:4320/v1/opamp"
                 Sequence="ui"
                 After="LaunchConditions"
                 Condition="NOT ENDPOINT AND NOT Installed" />
    <Property Id="WIXUI_INSTALLDIR" Value="INSTALLFOLDER" Secure="yes" />

    <!--
      One directory the operator configures (ADR-0019 clause 39), holding the payload this package
      delivers: `supervisor.exe` and nothing else. The layout and the state directory are not here
      — `service install` puts them under `%ProgramData%\opamp-fleet`, for ADR-0019 clause 8's
      reason. The operator still configures one path; it simply is not the install root.

      Named after the **product**, not its display name (ADR-0019 clause 7): `nodejs`, `Git` and
      `PowerShell` sit under `Program Files` under their own slugs, and a directory named for the
      display name would be the one place a variant build could not tell itself apart.
    -->
    <StandardDirectory Id="ProgramFiles64Folder">
      <Directory Id="INSTALLFOLDER" Name="opamp-fleet" />
    </StandardDirectory>

    <ComponentGroup Id="ClientFiles" Directory="INSTALLFOLDER">
      <Component Id="ClientExe">
        <File Id="SupervisorExe"
              Name="supervisor.exe"
              Source="$(BinaryPath)"
              KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
    </ComponentGroup>

    <Feature Id="Main" Title="OpAMP Fleet Agent" Level="1">
      <ComponentGroupRef Id="ClientFiles" />
    </Feature>

    <!--
      WelcomeDlg → InstallDirDlg → EndpointDlg → VerifyReadyDlg (WixUI_OpampInstallDir.wxs).
      A plain UIRef rather than `ui:WixUI`: that element names one of the toolset's built-in sets,
      and this set is ours.
    -->
    <UIRef Id="WixUI_OpampInstallDir" />

    <!--
      Registering the service, deferred and without impersonation: it writes under Program Files and
      talks to the SCM, and neither is something the invoking user may be able to do. A type 18
      custom action takes a command line that "commonly contains properties that are designated
      dynamically" — the Target is a formatted field resolved when the installation script is
      written, so INSTALLFOLDER and ENDPOINT arrive without a CustomActionData round trip.

      Two actions rather than one condition inside a command line, because an empty endpoint flag
      is not the same request as no endpoint flag at all: the first would be rejected by the
      loader's own endpoint rule, the second is the ordinary "configure it later" install that
      only warns.

      The self-update answer needs no third and fourth action, because unlike the endpoint it *has*
      a no-op spelling: `[SELFUPDATEFLAG]` is empty when the consent stands, and a formatted field
      of an unset property resolves to nothing — so the consenting command line carries no
      self-update flag at all.

      **No root flag here, and that is ADR-0019 clause 8.** A root would name `[INSTALLFOLDER]`, and
      a directory property always resolves with a trailing backslash, which the C runtime that
      builds the client's argv reads before a quote as an escaped, literal quote rather than the
      end of the argument — so the root would swallow the rest of the line and the install would
      die with error 1722. INSTALLFOLDER is not the install root, so no directory property reaches
      a command line at all. `tests/msi_exe_command.rs` parses these lines exactly as the CRT will
      and asserts both: that they survive the split, and that they name no root.

      (Spelled without the flag's own name on purpose: an XML comment may not contain a double
      hyphen, so writing it out here is a build error, not a typo somebody can fix later.)
    -->
    <CustomAction Id="RegisterServiceWithEndpoint"
                  FileRef="SupervisorExe"
                  ExeCommand="service install --endpoint &quot;[ENDPOINT]&quot;[SELFUPDATEFLAG]"
                  Execute="deferred"
                  Impersonate="no"
                  Return="check" />

    <CustomAction Id="RegisterService"
                  FileRef="SupervisorExe"
                  ExeCommand="service install[SELFUPDATEFLAG]"
                  Execute="deferred"
                  Impersonate="no"
                  Return="check" />

    <!--
      The mirror, and best-effort by design (`Return="ignore"`): a host whose service was already
      unregistered by hand must still be able to uninstall. Sequenced before RemoveFiles, because a
      type 18 action needs its executable to still be on disk.

      `service uninstall` leaves both roots behind, as it does everywhere (ADR-0019) — deleting a
      supervisor.toml the operator answered or edited would be the overwrite ADR-0019 refuses, one
      step later. What this package's uninstaller then empties is INSTALLFOLDER, which
      holds only the payload it delivered: exactly right, and the reason ADR-0019 clause 8 moved
      the data out of `Program Files`. The configuration and the state survive under
      `%ProgramData%\opamp-fleet`, the same remove-versus-purge shape the `.deb` has.
    -->
    <CustomAction Id="StopService"
                  FileRef="SupervisorExe"
                  ExeCommand="service stop"
                  Execute="deferred"
                  Impersonate="no"
                  Return="ignore" />

    <CustomAction Id="UnregisterService"
                  FileRef="SupervisorExe"
                  ExeCommand="service uninstall"
                  Execute="deferred"
                  Impersonate="no"
                  Return="ignore" />

    <InstallExecuteSequence>
      <!--
        After InstallFiles: the executable these actions run is the one this package just laid down,
        so it has to exist first.

        The service is registered and left stopped, exactly as the `.deb` and `.rpm` post-install
        does and for the same reason (ADR-0019 clause 37): a Client with no configuration
        dials the development default and manages nothing, and an installer must not manufacture
        that state on every host it touches. An install that was given an ENDPOINT still does not
        start — the client identity is not in yet (ADR-0019).
      -->
      <Custom Action="RegisterServiceWithEndpoint" After="InstallFiles"
              Condition="NOT REMOVE AND ENDPOINT&lt;&gt;&quot;&quot;" />
      <Custom Action="RegisterService" After="RegisterServiceWithEndpoint"
              Condition="NOT REMOVE AND ENDPOINT=&quot;&quot;" />

      <!-- Only a real uninstall. On a major upgrade the new package re-registers straight after. -->
      <Custom Action="StopService" Before="UnregisterService"
              Condition="REMOVE=&quot;ALL&quot;" />
      <Custom Action="UnregisterService" Before="RemoveFiles"
              Condition="REMOVE=&quot;ALL&quot;" />
    </InstallExecuteSequence>

  </Package>
</Wix>
